sec.p:
When simulating neural activity responses for a continuous estimation task, the response was generated according to the following model

(1)

f(θ)=Aexp⁡(κcos⁡(θ−θpref))2πI0(κ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f(\theta)=\frac{A \exp \left(\kappa \cos \left(\theta-\theta_{\text {pref }}\right)\right)}{2 \pi I_{0}(\kappa)} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(2)

f(θ)=gf(θ+δ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f(\theta)=g f(\theta+\delta) \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(3)

ri(θ)∼Pois(fi(θ)).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{i}(\theta) \sim \operatorname{Pois}\left(f_{i}(\theta)\right) . \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

When generating simulated data for binary classification tasks, the neural response was generated according to the following expressions:

(4)

r→(θi)∼N(f(θ→i),Σ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \vec{r}\left(\theta_{i}\right) \sim \mathcal{N}\left(f\left(\vec{\theta}_{i}\right), \Sigma\right) \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(5)

Σ=Σ0+ϵf→′f→′T
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Sigma=\Sigma_{0}+\epsilon \vec{f}^{\prime} \vec{f}^{\primeT} \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(6)

f→′=f→(θ1)−f→(θ2)Δθ.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \vec{f}^{\prime}=\frac{\vec{f}\left(\theta_{1}\right)-\vec{f}\left(\theta_{2}\right)}{\Delta \theta} . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Assume that a one-dimensional stimulus variable θ is represented by a neural population code with N neurons (N may approach infinity). Denote the tuning curve of the ith neuron as fi(θ), which is a smooth function. fi(θ) does not need to be Gaussian function. We will assume that neurons exhibit Poisson spiking noise, although the arguments below may also work for other types of noise (such as negative binomial noise or Gaussian noise). Thus, the code can be expressed as the following:
ri∼Polsson(fi(θ)Δt) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_{i} \sim \operatorname{Polsson}\left(f_{i}(\theta) \Delta t\right)$$\end{document}
where ri represents the activity of the ith neuron.

We model information-limiting noise by injecting Gaussian noise to the stimulus
ri∼Poisson(fi(θ+δ)Δt) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_{i} \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(f_{i}(\theta+\delta) \Delta t\right)$$\end{document}
where the random variable δ denotes information-limiting noise and is shared by all neurons. Note that, in this model, noise correlations are only induced by information-limiting noise. The Poisson spiking noise is private to individual neurons. Although this setting is likely simplified, as in practice there may exist other types of shared neural variability that are not information-limiting, our simulation results in Fig. 1 show that it is already challenging to use standard decoding methods to estimate information-limiting noise.

Assume that information-limiting noise δ is small and the tuning curve is smooth, given a sufficient amount of data, the tuning curves for individual neurons can be accurately estimated. For a sufficiently large neural population, the Bayesian estimator based on each split, θ^i(i=1,2)\begin{document}$\hat{\theta}_{i}(\mathrm{i}=1,2)$\end{document}, is expected to be approximately Gaussian distributed [54], centered at θ + δ. The reason is because Bayesian decoding in this case is essentially decoding θ + δ from a large neural population with independent neural noise. Thus, we can write
θ1^=θ+δ+ϕ1,θ2^=θ+δ+ϕ2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{\theta_{1}}=\theta+\delta+\phi_{1}, \hat{\theta_{2}}=\theta+\delta+\phi_{2}$$\end{document}
where ϕ1 and ϕ2 represent the decoding error of θ + δ for the two splits. Furthermore, because that neural noise is independent in the neural population (when treating the decoding problem as one that decodes θ+δ), the decoding error from the two splits should be independent. Therefore, under the above assumptions, ϕ1 and ϕ2 are independent random variables. Given these, a straightforward calculation (as shown in the Methods section) shows that the covariance of θ1−θ1\begin{document}$\theta_{1}-\theta_{1}$\end{document} and θ2−θ2\begin{document}$\theta_{2}-\theta_{2}$\end{document} can be used to estimate the variance of information-limiting noise δ.

First, we will investigate the mean and variance in the error of the locally optimal linear estimator (LOLE) [55, 5]. We will use these results for further calculations and for assessment of the split-trial technique. Note that by using the locally optimal linear estimator, we are assuming that the decoder has knowledge of the noise correlation structure that is induced by non-information-limiting noise. The locally optimal linear estimator for θ\begin{document}$\theta$\end{document} can be written as:


θ^=θ+f→′(θ)TΣ−1(θ)(x→−f→(θ))f→′(θ)TΣ−1(θ)f→′(θ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{\theta}=\theta+\frac{\vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta)^{T} \Sigma^{-1}(\theta)(\vec{x}-\vec{f}(\theta))}{\vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta)^{T} \Sigma^{-1}(\theta) \vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta)}$$\end{document}



known to be optimal when estimating a parameter value near θ [55, 5]. Starting with the denominator of the fraction:


f→′(θ)TΣ−1(θ)f→′(θ)=f′Tdiag(σi−2)f′=∑i=1nfi′(θ)2σi2.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta)^{T} \Sigma^{-1}(\theta) \vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta) & =f^{\prime T} \operatorname{diag}\left(\sigma_{i}^{-2}\right) f^{\prime} \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{f_{i}^{\prime}(\theta)^{2}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

We evaluate the numerator similarly:


f→′(θ)TΣ−1(θ)(x→−f→(θ))=f′Tdiag(σi−2)(x→−f→(θ))=[f1′(θ)/σ12⋮fn′(θ)/σn2]⋅([x1⋮xn]−[f1(θ)⋮fn(θ)])=∑i=1nxifi′(θ)σi2−∑i=1nfi(θ)fi′(θ)σi2=∑i=1n(xi−fi(θ))fi′(θ)σi2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \vec{f}^{\prime}(\theta)^{T} \Sigma^{-1}(\theta)(\vec{x}-\vec{f}(\theta)) & =f^{\prime T} \operatorname{diag}\left(\sigma_{i}^{-2}\right)(\vec{x}-\vec{f}(\theta)) \\ & =\left[\begin{array}{c}f_{1}^{\prime}(\theta) / \sigma_{1}^{2} \\ \vdots \\ f_{n}^{\prime}(\theta) / \sigma_{n}^{2}\end{array}\right] \cdot\left(\left[\begin{array}{c}x_{1} \\ \vdots \\ x_{n}\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{c}f_{1}(\theta) \\ \vdots \\ f_{n}(\theta)\end{array}\right]\right) \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{x_{i} f_{i}^{\prime}(\theta)}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}-\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{f_{i}(\theta) f_{i}^{\prime}(\theta)}{\sigma_{i}^{2}} \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\left(x_{i}-f_{i}(\theta)\right) f_{i}^{\prime}(\theta)}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

We can then combine terms and evaluate the decoding error:


θ^−θ=∑i=1n(xi−fi(θ))fi′(θ)σi2∑i=1nfi′(θ)2σi2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{\theta}-\theta=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\left(x_{i}-f_{i}(\theta)\right) f_{i}^{\prime}(\theta)}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{f_{i}^{\prime}(\theta)^{2}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}}$$\end{document}

We proceed with the variance calculation:


Var[θ^−θ]=E[(θ^−θ)2]−E[θ^−θ]2=E[(θ^−θ)2]=E[(∑i=1n(xi−fi)fi′σi2∑i=1nfi′2σi2)2]=E[∑i=1n((xi−fi)fi′σi2)2+2∑i<jn(xi−fi)(xj−fj)fi′fj′σi2σj2](f′TΣ−1f′)2.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}-\theta] & =\mathrm{E}\left[(\hat{\theta}-\theta)^{2}\right]-\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}-\theta]^{2} \\ & =\mathrm{E}\left[(\hat{\theta}-\theta)^{2}\right] \\ & =\mathrm{E}\left[\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\left(x_{i}-f_{i}\right) f_{i}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}}\right)^{2}\right] \\ & =\frac{\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{\left(x_{i}-f_{i}\right) f_{i}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}+2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\left(x_{i}-f_{i}\right)\left(x_{j}-f_{j}\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}}\right]}{\left(f^{\prime T} \Sigma^{-1} f^{\prime}\right)^{2}} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

We’ll evaluate each term individually starting with the diagonal term in the numerator:


E[∑i=1n((xi−fi)fi′σi2)2]=E[∑i=1nxi2fi′2−2xififi′2+fi2fi′2σi4]=∑i=1nE[xi2]fi′2−2E[xi]fifi′2+fi2fi′2σi4=∑i=1nE[xi2]fi′2−fi2fi′2σi4=∑i=1n(σi2+fi2)fi′2−fi2fi′2σi4=∑i=1nσi2fi′2σi4=∑i=1nfi′2σi2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} E\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{\left(x_{i}-f_{i}\right) f_{i}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}\right] & =\mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{x_{i}^{2} f_{i}^{\prime 2}-2 x_{i} f_{i} f_{i}^{\prime 2}+f_{i}^{2} f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{4}}\right] \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\mathrm{E}\left[x_{i}^{2}\right] f_{i}^{\prime 2}-2 \mathrm{E}\left[x_{i}\right] f_{i} f_{i}^{\prime 2}+f_{i}^{2} f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{4}} \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\mathrm{E}\left[x_{i}^{2}\right] f_{i}^{\prime 2}-f_{i}^{2} f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{4}} \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\left(\sigma_{i}^{2}+f_{i}^{2}\right) f_{i}^{\prime 2}-f_{i}^{2} f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{4}} \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{\sigma_{i}^{2} f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{4}} \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

Now we address the numerator cross-term:


[∑i<jn(xi−fi)(xj−fj)fi′fj′σi2σj2]=2E[∑i<jnxixjfi′fj′−xifjfi′fj′−xjfifi′fj′+fifjfi′fj′σi2σj2]=2∑i<jnE[xixj]fi′fj−E[xi]fjfi′fj′−E[xj]fifi′fj′+fifjfi′fj′σi2σj2=2∑i<jn(Cov(xi,xj)+E[xi]E[xj])fi′fj′−fifjfi′fj′σi2σj2=2∑i<jn(Cov(xi,xj)+fifj)fi′fj′−fifjfi′fj′σi2σj2=2∑i<jnCov(xi,xj)fi′fj′σi2σj2.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {\left[\sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\left(x_{i}-f_{i}\right)\left(x_{j}-f_{j}\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}}\right] } & =2 \mathrm{E}\left[\sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{x_{i} x_{j} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}-x_{i} f_{j} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}-x_{j} f_{i} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}+f_{i} f_{j} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}}\right] \\ & =2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\mathrm{E}\left[x_{i} x_{j}\right] f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}-\mathrm{E}\left[x_{i}\right] f_{j} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}-\mathrm{E}\left[x_{j}\right] f_{i} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}+f_{i} f_{j} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}} \\ & =2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\left(\operatorname{Cov}\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right)+\mathrm{E}\left[x_{i}\right] \mathrm{E}\left[x_{j}\right]\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}-f_{i} f_{j} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}} \\ & =2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\left(\operatorname{Cov}\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right)+f_{i} f_{j}\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}-f_{i} f_{j} f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}} \\ & =2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\operatorname{Cov}\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

So we have that


Var[θ^−θ]=∑i=1nfi′2σi2+2∑i<jnCov(xi,xj)fi′fj′σi2σj2(f′TΣ−1f′)2=f→TΣ−1f→′+2∑i<jnCov(xi,xj)fi′fj′σi2σj2(f′TΣ−1f′)2=1f→TTΣ−1f→′+2∑i<jnCov(xi,xj)fi′fj′σi2σj2(f′TΣ−1f′)2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}-\theta] & =\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{f_{i}^{\prime 2}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}+2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\operatorname{Cov}\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}}}{\left(f^{\prime T} \Sigma^{-1} f^{\prime}\right)^{2}} \\ & =\frac{\vec{f}^{T} \Sigma^{-1} \vec{f}^{\prime}+2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\operatorname{Cov}\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}}}{\left(f^{\prime T} \Sigma^{-1} f^{\prime}\right)^{2}} \\ & =\frac{1}{\vec{f}^{T} T \Sigma^{-1} \vec{f}^{\prime}}+\frac{2 \sum_{i<j}^{n} \frac{\operatorname{Cov}\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right) f_{i}^{\prime} f_{j}^{\prime}}{\sigma_{i}^{2} \sigma_{j}^{2}}}{\left(f^{\prime T} \Sigma^{-1} f^{\prime}\right)^{2}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

We will start with special case for which Σ0=σ¯I egin{document}$\Sigma_{0}= ar{\sigma} \mathbb{I}$\end{document}, a diagonal matrix. In this case, all noise correlations are caused by information-limiting noise. Recall I=f′T→Σ−1f′→ egin{document}$I=\overrightarrow{f^{\prime} T} \Sigma^{-1} \overrightarrow{f^{\prime}}$\end{document} is the linear Fisher information for a population with tuning curves f→ egin{document}$\vec{f}$\end{document} and covariance Σ.
Cov(Y,Z)=E[YZ]−E[Y]E[Z]=E[YZ]=1IYIZ∑j=1n∑i=1nCov(yi,zj)gi′hj′ηi2νj2=1IYIZ∑j=1n∑i=1nϵgi′hj′gi′hj′ηi2νj2=ϵIYIZ∑j=1n∑i=1ngi′2hj′2ηi2νj2=ϵ  egin{document}$$\displaystyle  egin{aligned} \operatorname{Cov}(Y, Z) & =\mathrm{E}[Y Z]-\mathrm{E}[Y] \mathrm{E}[Z] \ & =\mathrm{E}[Y Z] \ & = rac{1}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n}  rac{\operatorname{Cov}\left(y_{i}, z_{j}ight) g_{i}^{\prime} h_{j}^{\prime}}{\eta_{i}^{2} 
u_{j}^{2}} \ & = rac{1}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n}  rac{\epsilon g_{i}^{\prime} h_{j}^{\prime} g_{i}^{\prime} h_{j}^{\prime}}{\eta_{i}^{2} 
u_{j}^{2}} \ & = rac{\epsilon}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n}  rac{g_{i}^{\prime 2} h_{j}^{\prime 2}}{\eta_{i}^{2} 
u_{j}^{2}} \ & =\epsilon\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
which confirms the validity of our estimator in the case where information-limiting noise is added to an otherwise independent population, in the case of a population of neurons with Gaussian tuning curve and Gaussian noise. These results complementary to the results reported in Section B.

In this section, we will relax assumptions on Σ0 and examine the general case. Specially, we allow other forms of noise correlations that are not caused by information-limiting noise. Let kijY\begin{document}$k_{i j}^{Y}$\end{document} be the element of ΣY−1\begin{document}$\Sigma_{Y}^{-1}$\end{document} in the ith row and jth column and C0,ij be the element in the ith row and jth column of Σ0.


Cov(Y,Z)=E[(g→′TΣY−1(y→−g→)g→′TΣY−1g′→)(h→TΣZ−1(z→−h→)h→′TΣZ−1h′→)]=1IYIZE[(g→TΣY−1(y→−g→))(h→′TΣZ−1(z→−h→))]=1IYIZ∑l=1n∑k=1n∑j=1n∑i=1nCov(yj,zl)gi′hk′kijYkklZ=1IYIZ∑l=1n∑k=1n∑j=1n∑i=1n(Cjl+ϵgj′hl′)gi′hk′kijYkklZ=ϵIYIZ∑l=1n∑k=1n∑j=1n∑i=1ngj′kijYgi′hk′kklZhl′+1IYIZ∑l=1n∑k=1n∑j=1n∑i=1nCjlgi′hk′kijYkklZ=ϵ+1IYIZ∑l=1n∑k=1n∑j=1n∑i=1nC0,jlgi′hk′kijYkklZ=ϵ+g→TΣY−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′IYIZ.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \operatorname{Cov}(Y, Z) & =\mathrm{E}\left[\left(\frac{\vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1}(\vec{y}-\vec{g})}{\vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1} \overrightarrow{g^{\prime}}}\right)\left(\frac{\vec{h}^{T} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1}(\vec{z}-\vec{h})}{\vec{h}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \overrightarrow{h^{\prime}}}\right)\right] \\ & =\frac{1}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \mathrm{E}\left[\left(\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1}(\vec{y}-\vec{g})\right)\left(\vec{h}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1}(\vec{z}-\vec{h})\right)\right] \\ & =\frac{1}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{l=1}^{n} \sum_{k=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \operatorname{Cov}\left(y_{j}, z_{l}\right) g_{i}^{\prime} h_{k}^{\prime} k_{i j}^{Y} k_{k l}^{Z} \\ & =\frac{1}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{l=1}^{n} \sum_{k=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(C_{j l}+\epsilon g_{j}^{\prime} h_{l}^{\prime}\right) g_{i}^{\prime} h_{k}^{\prime} k_{i j}^{Y} k_{k l}^{Z} \\ & =\frac{\epsilon}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{l=1}^{n} \sum_{k=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} g_{j}^{\prime} k_{i j}^{Y} g_{i}^{\prime} h_{k}^{\prime} k_{k l}^{Z} h_{l}^{\prime}+\frac{1}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{l=1}^{n} \sum_{k=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} C_{j l} g_{i}^{\prime} h_{k}^{\prime} k_{i j}^{Y} k_{k l}^{Z} \\ & =\epsilon+\frac{1}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} \sum_{l=1}^{n} \sum_{k=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} C_{0, j l} g_{i}^{\prime} h_{k}^{\prime} k_{i j}^{Y} k_{k l}^{Z} \\ & =\epsilon+\frac{\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

In this section we are going to examine an alternate form to our error term and from which derive some useful properties of the split-trial analysis. We will begin with establishing some quick identities for linear fisher information, using IY as an example. Proceeding from the assumption that ΣY = Σ0,Y + ϵg′g′T, we can use the Sherman-Morrison formula to obtain:


ΣY−1=Σ0,Y−1−Σ0,Y−1ϵg→′g→′TΣ0,Y−11+ϵg→′TΣ0,Y−1g→′⟹IY=g→TΣY−1g→′=g→TΣ0,Y−1g→′−ϵ(g→TΣ0,Y−1g→′)(g→TΣ0,Y−1g→′)1+ϵg→′TΣ0,Y−1g→′=I0,Y−ϵI0,Y21+ϵI0,Y=I0,Y1+ϵI0,Y
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1} & =\Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1}-\frac{\Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \epsilon \vec{g}^{\prime} \vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1}}{1+\epsilon \vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime}} \\ \Longrightarrow I_{Y}=\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime} & =\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime}-\frac{\epsilon\left(\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime}\right)\left(\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime}\right)}{1+\epsilon \vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime}} \\ & =I_{0, Y}-\frac{\epsilon I_{0, Y}^{2}}{1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}} \\ & =\frac{I_{0, Y}}{1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



as was similarly presented in [5]. We can now return to our error term with these calculations in mind. Specifically, we can say:


g→TΣY−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′=g′→[Σ0,Y−1−Σ0,Y−1ϵg→′g→TΣ0,Y−11+ϵg→′TΣ0,Y−1g→′]Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′−ϵ(g→TΣ0,Y−1g→′)g→TΣ0,Y−11+ϵI0,YΣ0,YZΣZ−1h→′=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′−(ϵI0,Y1+ϵI0,Y)g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′(1−ϵI0,Y1+ϵI0,Y)=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′(11+ϵI0,Y)=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′1+ϵI0,Y.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime} & =\overrightarrow{g^{\prime}}\left[\Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1}-\frac{\Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \epsilon \vec{g}^{\prime} \vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1}}{1+\epsilon \vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime}}\right] \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime} \\ & =\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}-\frac{\epsilon\left(\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \vec{g}^{\prime}\right) \vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1}}{1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime} \\ & =\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}-\left(\frac{\epsilon I_{0, Y}}{1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}}\right) \vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime} \\ & =\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}\left(1-\frac{\epsilon I_{0, Y}}{1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}}\right) \\ & =\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}\left(\frac{1}{1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}}\right) \\ & =\frac{\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

A similar argument using ΣZ−1\begin{document}$\Sigma_{Z}^{-1}$\end{document} gives us that


g→TΣY−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1h→′(1+ϵI0,Y)(1+ϵI0,Z)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}=\frac{\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{\left(1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}\right)\left(1+\epsilon I_{0, Z}\right)}$$\end{document}

Now we can use the previously established substitutions for IY, IZ established above to write


g→′TΣY−1Σ0,YZΣZ−1h→′IYIZ=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1h→′(1+ϵI0,Y)(1+ϵI0,Z)⋅(1+ϵI0,Y)(1+ϵI0,Z)I0,YI0,Z=g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1h→′I0,YI0,Z.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{I_{Y} I_{Z}} & =\frac{\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{\left(1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}\right)\left(1+\epsilon I_{0, Z}\right)} \cdot \frac{\left(1+\epsilon I_{0, Y}\right)\left(1+\epsilon I_{0, Z}\right)}{I_{0, Y} I_{0, Z}} \\ & =\frac{\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{I_{0, Y} I_{0, Z}} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

We first will rewrite g→′TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1h→′\begin{document}$\vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}$\end{document} as g→′TΣ0,Y−1/2Σ0,Y−1/2Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1/2Σ0,Z−1/2h→′\begin{document}$\vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1 / 2} \vec{h}^{\prime}$\end{document}. We can then obtain
g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1h→′∣=|g→TΣ0,Y−1/2Σ0,Y−1/2Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1/2Σ0,Z−1/2h→′|≤‖Σ0,Y−1/2Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1/2‖op⋅‖Σ0,Y−1/2g→′‖⋅‖Σ0,Z−1/2h→′‖≤‖Σ0,Y−1/2Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1/2‖op⋅I0,Y⋅I0,Z≤ρmax⋅Ihalf \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime} \mid & =\left|\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1 / 2} \vec{h}^{\prime}\right| \\ & \leq\left\|\Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1 / 2}\right\|_{o p} \cdot\left\|\Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1 / 2} \vec{g}^{\prime}\right\| \cdot\left\|\Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1 / 2} \vec{h}^{\prime}\right\| \\ & \leq\left\|\Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1 / 2} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1 / 2}\right\|_{o p} \cdot \sqrt{I_{0, Y}} \cdot \sqrt{I_{0, Z}} \\ & \leq \rho_{\max } \cdot I_{\text {half }}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where the second line is an application of Cauchy-Schwarz,|| ||op is the operator norm, and ρmax is the maximum canonical correlation between subpopulations Y and Z. The equivalence between the canonical correlations and the operator norm in the last line comes from established results on CCA and singular values as shown in [56]. From here we can establish |g→TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1h→′Ihalf|≤ρmax≤1\begin{document}$\left.\frac{\vec{g}^{T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{I_{\text {half }}} \right\rvert\, \leq \rho_{\max } \leq 1$\end{document} and we conclude −1≤g→′TΣ0,Y−1Σ0,YZΣ0,Z−1h→′Ihalf≤1\begin{document}$-1 \leq \frac{\vec{g}^{\prime T} \Sigma_{0, Y}^{-1} \Sigma_{0, Y Z} \Sigma_{0, Z}^{-1} \vec{h}^{\prime}}{I_{\text {half }}} \leq 1$\end{document}.
